{
  "description": "Two halves of one claim, because the legal half alone cannot detect the defect. `local name` \"declares one or more names in the current scope ... shadowing anything of that name that was visible\" (spec/commands.md:110, spec/execution-model.md:503-506), and a procedure's parameter is one of the three things its body can see (spec/execution-model.md:389-391), so `local a` beside a parameter `:a` is legal and creates a NEW binding. `f` assigns that binding and prints `99` — the positive. `g` reads it without assigning and raises `ol-undefined-var` — the proof that the declaration really did create a second, unbound binding rather than quietly reusing the parameter, which is the one observation that distinguishes the two implementations: with the parameter reused, `g` would print `1` and `f` would still print `99`.",
